.TH CCOL_MEMPOOL_DYNAMIC_ALLOCS_COUNT 3 "2026-09-15" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_mempool_dynamic_allocs_count \- report outstanding fallback allocations
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cmempool.h>

size_t ccol_mempool_dynamic_allocs_count(ccol_mempool *mp);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Returns the number of allocations currently outstanding that came from
the system allocator rather than from the pool's own storage. These occur
only in a pool created with fallback_to_dynamic_memory = true, and only
once the pool's own elements are exhausted.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
mp                        Memory pool to query (must not be NULL)
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns the number of outstanding fallback allocations, or 0 for a pool
created without fallback
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
A non-zero result means the pool was too small for its workload at some
point. It is the signal to raise elem_count: every fallback allocation is
a system allocator round trip, which is the cost the pool exists to
avoid.

Entries held in a per-thread cache are pool-owned and are never counted
here.

ccol_mempool_destroy() treats outstanding fallback allocations as a leak
and terminates the process, so a pool with fallback enabled must have
every fallback entry freed before it is destroyed.
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
Thread-safe when the pool was created with single_threaded = false.
Acquires the pool's lock, so it is a cold-path call and not intended for
use inside a hot loop.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_mempool_total_capacity (3),
.BR ccol_mempool_used_count (3),
.BR ccol_mempool_create (3)
